    <h1>OEM &amp; Private Label: What the Terms Mean</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      This page explains the concepts of OEM and private-label manufacturing as they apply to
      incense, in neutral terms. It is a knowledge explainer, not a service page — there is nothing
      to order here.
    </p>

    <h2>The distinction in one line</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>OEM</strong> — a product is built <em>to your specification</em>: your formula, format, and packaging.</li>
      <li><strong>Private label</strong> — you take an <em>existing</em> product and apply your own branding.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The deciding question is simple: <em>do you have a product in mind, or only a brand?</em> If
      the product exists because you briefed it, that is OEM; if it would exist even if you never
      showed up, that is private label. The full comparison lives in the
      <a href="/blog/oem-vs-private-label/">OEM vs private label</a> explainer.
    </p>

    <h2>What the craft covers</h2>
    <p>
      Both routes sit on the same craft, documented across the knowledge base:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Blended incense (合香) — see the <a href="/blog/hexiang-blending-system/">blending system</a>.</li>
      <li>Material identity and sourcing — see the <a href="/ingredients/">ingredient reference</a> and the <a href="/blog/incense-substitution-cross-reference/">substitution cross-reference</a>.</li>
      <li>Product formats (stick, cone, coil, sachet, raw material).</li>
      <li>Quality control — incoming, in-process, and final inspection, described in <a href="/blog/how-incense-is-made/">how incense is made</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Questions a knowledgeable maker asks</h2>
    <p>
      OEM and private-label work succeeds or fails on evidence, not promises. Four checks recur
      across the craft:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Sample against the brief.</strong> A sample verifies the product — aroma, format, material, and burn.</li>
      <li><strong>Agree the specification.</strong> The written spec is what every later batch is checked against.</li>
      <li><strong>Request and verify documentation.</strong> Certificates and test reports are claims to verify, not badges to assume.</li>
      <li><strong>Ask how quality control works.</strong> Incoming, in-process, and final inspection are the process a maker should be able to describe concretely.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      For the deeper treatment, read the <a href="/blog/oem-vs-private-label/">OEM vs private label</a>
      comparison and the <a href="/blog/incense-manufacturing-quality-control/">quality control</a> explainer.
    </p>
